BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mats-residency-program-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T150945Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: MATS Residency Program: 6-24 Month Funded AI Safety Resea
 rch (Winter 2027 intake)
DESCRIPTION:The MATS Residency\, announced in August 2026 as a new track al
 ongside the seasonal MATS fellowship\, funds 6 to 24 months of high-indepe
 ndence AI safety research for experienced researchers\, whether already in
  AI safety or moving in from adjacent fields.\n\nOrganisation: MATS (ML Al
 ignment and Theory Scholars)\nLocation: In-person at MATS offices in Berke
 ley\, London\, or Washington DC\; visa support provided where required. Fo
 r experienced researchers with at least one substantial piece of research-
 caliber work.\nAward: USD 155\,000 to 285\,000 per year gross (set by loca
 tion\, seniority\, and experience)\, plus medical/dental/vision/life insur
 ance\, retirement plan\, paid time off\, conference funding\, catered meal
 s\, and up to USD 25\,000 relocation support. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: 6 to 24 months with rolling start dates\; three intake windows per year
 .\n\nMore: https://www.matsprogram.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouv
 eaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.matsprogram.org/residency?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrant
 desk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:In-person at MATS offices in Berkeley\, London\, or Washington DC\
 ; visa support provided where required. For experienced researchers with a
 t least one substantial piece of research-caliber work.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:brooks-international-fellowship-tate-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T150945Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Brooks International Fellowship Programme 2027 (Tate Brit
 ain x Delfina Foundation)
DESCRIPTION:Tate\, in collaboration with Delfina Foundation\, invites appli
 cations for the thirteenth Brooks International Fellowship Programme: two 
 6-month fellowships in 2027 for international researchers based outside th
 e UK\, hosted in the Tate Britain Curatorial Division and complemented by 
 activities and residency life at Delfina Foundation.\n\nOrganisation: Tate
 \, in collaboration with Delfina Foundation\nLocation: London\, UK (workin
 g at Tate Britain\, living at Delfina Foundation). Open to international r
 esearchers based outside the UK.\nAward: Fully funded 6-month fellowship: 
 living allowance bursary of GBP 40 per day\, single bedroom at Delfina Fou
 ndation (house of up to 8 residents with shared facilities)\, return econo
 my flight\, UK airport transfers\, visa fee reimbursement with visa suppor
 t\, London Underground and bus travel pass\, a research resource allowance
 \, and travel to other Tate galleries (Tate Modern\, Liverpool\, St Ives) 
 and other UK and European cultural institutions. Two fellowships available
 . No application fee.\nDuration: Six months\, 5 April to 17 September 2027
 \n\nMore: https://jobsearch.tate.org.uk/jobs/job/Brooks-International-Fell
 ow-Programme/839?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://jobsearch.tate.org.uk/jobs/job/Brooks-International-Fellow-Prog
 ramme/839?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London\, UK (working at Tate Britain\, living at Delfina Foundatio
 n). Open to international researchers based outside the UK.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tate-delfina-brooks-international-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T150945Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261021
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261022
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tate x Delfina Foundation: Brooks International Fellowshi
 p Programme 2027 (London)
DESCRIPTION:Now in its thirteenth year\, the Brooks International Fellowshi
 p Programme places international researchers within Tate Britain's Curator
 ial Division for six months\, with accommodation and a parallel residency 
 programme at Delfina Foundation.\n\nOrganisation: Tate and Delfina Foundat
 ion\nLocation: Tate Britain Curatorial Division\, London\, UK\, with accom
 modation and complementary activities at Delfina Foundation. Open to inter
 national researchers based outside the UK.\nAward: Non-salaried but fully 
 supported: return economy-class flight\, UK airport transfers\, visa fee r
 eimbursement and visa support via Delfina Foundation\, a living allowance 
 bursary of GBP 40 per day\, a London Underground and bus travel pass\, a s
 ingle bedroom at Delfina Foundation with shared facilities\, and travel to
  other Tate galleries and UK/European cultural institutions. The Leonora C
 arrington fellowship adds a research resource allowance. No application fe
 e.\nDuration: Six months\, 5 April to 17 September 2027.\n\nMore: https://
 www.delfinafoundation.com/open-calls/current/open-call-with-tate-for-brook
 s-international-fellowship-programme-2027/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.delfinafoundation.com/open-calls/current/open-call-with-tat
 e-for-brooks-international-fellowship-programme-2027/?utm_source=artificia
 lnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Tate Britain Curatorial Division\, London\, UK\, with accommodatio
 n and complementary activities at Delfina Foundation. Open to internationa
 l researchers based outside the UK.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:pivotal-research-fellowship-2027-q1@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T150945Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Pivotal Research Fellowship (2027 Q1)
DESCRIPTION:Fully funded\, full-time\, in-person research fellowship in Lon
 don for anyone 18 or over committed to ensuring AI develops safely. Fellow
 s work on a project with an established mentor\, with weekly 1-on-1s\, ded
 icated research-management support and co-working at LISA. Past projects s
 pan AI governance\, technical AI safety and technical AI governance.\n\nOr
 ganisation: Pivotal Research\nLocation: London (London Initiative for Safe
  AI / LISA). In person\; meeting UK entry requirements is the fellow's res
 ponsibility\, though Pivotal offers visa guidance and can sometimes help.\
 nAward: £15\,000 to £18\,000 stipend (£18\,000 for senior fellows\, e.g
 . PhD\, industry track record or strong publications) + travel to and from
  London + £1\,800/month housing for non-London fellows + weekday lunch an
 d dinner + compute.\nDuration: 15 weeks full-time (18 January to 30 April 
 2027)\n\nMore: https://www.pivotal-research.org/fellowships/ai-safety-fell
 owship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.pivotal-research.org/fellowships/ai-safety-fellowship?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:London (London Initiative for Safe AI / LISA). In person\; meeting
  UK entry requirements is the fellow's responsibility\, though Pivotal off
 ers visa guidance and can sometimes help.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tarbell-fellowship-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T150945Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261122
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261123
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tarbell Fellowship 2027-28 (AI journalism)
DESCRIPTION:A year-long fellowship for journalists covering artificial inte
 lligence. Fellows complete a three-month full-time remote programme on AI 
 and journalism fundamentals\, attend a weeklong summit\, then work for nin
 e months as reporters embedded in a partner newsroom (current partners are
  in Hong Kong\, London\, Los Angeles\, New York\, San Francisco\, Seattle\
 , Washington DC and Cambridge).\n\nOrganisation: Tarbell Center for AI Jou
 rnalism\nLocation: Remote training\, then newsroom placement in the US\, U
 K or Hong Kong. In person.\nAward: USD 75\,000 to 120\,000 depending on ex
 perience and placement cost of living\; senior fellows (7+ years experienc
 e) USD 115\,000 to 135\,000. Summit travel and accommodation covered. No a
 pplication fee.\nDuration: 12 months: remote training 7 June to 6 August 2
 027\, in-person summit August 2027\, newsroom placement September 2027 to 
 May 2028 (extendable by one year).\n\nMore: https://www.tarbellcenter.org/
 fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.tarbellcenter.org/fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote training\, then newsroom placement in the US\, UK or Hong K
 ong. In person.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:alpaca-festival-2027-open-call@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T150945Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261102
SUMMARY:Deadline: Alpaca Festival 2027: Open Call for Pattern-Based Perform
 ances\, Workshops and Talks (Sheffield\, UK)
DESCRIPTION:Alpaca is a festival and conference on algorithmic patterns in 
 the creative arts\, run by the non-profit Pattern Club in Sheffield. It br
 ings together live coders\, weavers\, braiders\, origami makers\, musician
 s and other practitioners who make patterns by procedure\, and it welcomes
  contemporary algorithmic art that is human-authored.\n\nOrganisation: Pat
 tern Club (Alpaca Festival)\nLocation: Sheffield\, UK\, with some talks an
 d activities online. Open internationally\, but only national (UK) travel 
 is covered\, plus a small budget for European train travel.\nAward: Perfor
 mances: artist fee plus national travel expenses. Workshops: artist fee\, 
 materials expenses and national travel expenses. Fee amounts are not yet c
 onfirmed (tight budget)\; additional costs may be covered on request. Inte
 rnational travel is not covered beyond a small European train budget. Conf
 erence talks: no fee or expenses\, in-person speakers get a free festival 
 weekend pass and lunch on 6 and 7 May. No application fee.\nDuration: Fest
 ival 6 to 9 May 2027: conference talks 6 and 7 May\, workshops and perform
 ances 7 to 9 May. Decisions expected December 2026.\n\nMore: https://alpac
 a.cc/call/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://alpaca.cc/call/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
LOCATION:Sheffield\, UK\, with some talks and activities online. Open inter
 nationally\, but only national (UK) travel is covered\, plus a small budge
 t for European train travel.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:braid-creative-commissions-a-promise-in-return-2027@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T150945Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261102
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261103
SUMMARY:Deadline: BRAID Creative Commissions 2027: A Promise in Return (Res
 ponsible AI\, GBP 8\,000-12\,000\, Edinburgh Art Festival)
DESCRIPTION:BRAID\, the UKRI AHRC-funded Responsible AI programme at the Un
 iversity of Edinburgh\, is commissioning around seven creative works that 
 treat Responsible AI as a question of relationships\, commitments and obli
 gations rather than a technical checklist or a set of rules\, under the ti
 tle A Promise in Return.\n\nOrganisation: BRAID (Bridging Responsible AI D
 ivides)\, University of Edinburgh\, with Inspace\, the Institute for Desig
 n Informatics and the Edinburgh Futures Institute\nLocation: UK. For UK-ba
 sed creative practitioners\; work is presented in Edinburgh during the Edi
 nburgh Art Festival\, August 2027.\nAward: Around seven commissions with b
 udgets of GBP 8\,000 to 12\,000 each\, inclusive of VAT and all costs. No 
 application fee.\nDuration: Projects run January to August 2027\, presente
 d during the Edinburgh Art Festival in August 2027\, with possible further
  displays through 2028.\n\nMore: https://braiduk.org/creative-commissions?
 utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://braiduk.org/creative-commissions?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:UK. For UK-based creative practitioners\; work is presented in Edi
 nburgh during the Edinburgh Art Festival\, August 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:daiwa-foundation-small-grants-march-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T150945Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270331
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270401
SUMMARY:Deadline: Daiwa Foundation Small Grants\, March 2027 round (GBP 2\,
 000-9\,000 for UK-Japan projects)
DESCRIPTION:The Daiwa Foundation Small Grants support projects that promote
  interaction between the UK and Japan in any field\, including arts exchan
 ges\, exhibitions\, conferences\, grassroots and educational exchanges and
  research travel\, with travel between the two countries as the main focus
 .\n\nOrganisation: Daiwa Anglo-Japanese Foundation\nLocation: For individu
 als\, societies\, associations and other bodies in the UK or Japan running
  projects that connect the two countries.\nAward: GBP 2\,000 to 9\,000 (ty
 pical awards GBP 3\,000-4\,000)\; flights up to GBP 1\,200 each and subsis
 tence up to GBP 100 per person per day. A contribution\, not full funding.
  No application fee.\nDuration: Per project\; activity can start from late
  May or early June 2027.\n\nMore: https://dajf.org.uk/grants-awards-and-pr
 izes/daiwa-foundation-small-grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://dajf.org.uk/grants-awards-and-prizes/daiwa-foundation-small-gra
 nts?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:For individuals\, societies\, associations and other bodies in the
  UK or Japan running projects that connect the two countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:costar-southbank-creative-intelligence-award-2027@artificialnouveau.git
 hub.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T150945Z
DTSTART:20261217T133000Z
DTEND:20261217T140000Z
SUMMARY:Deadline 14:00 GMT: The Creative Intelligence Award 2027 (CoSTAR Na
 tional Lab x Southbank Centre)
DESCRIPTION:Closes 17 Dec 2026 at 14:00 GMT.\n\nA new co-commission from Co
 STAR National Lab and Southbank Centre to take a creative technology idea 
 that is ready to be completed\, honed or technically advanced through focu
 sed R&D\, then present it to audiences at Southbank Centre's Creative Inte
 lligence festival in autumn 2027.\n\nOrganisation: CoSTAR National Lab and
  Southbank Centre\, supported by UKRI\nLocation: UK (lead applicant must b
 e a UK-registered company\; project activity UK-based). Prototyping at Pin
 ewood Studios\; showcase at Southbank Centre\, London.\nAward: GBP 50\,000
  grant (one award)\, plus up to GBP 25\,000 in AI compute and cloud credit
 s\, 2 x 5 days studio access at Pinewood Studios\, a six-week CoSTAR proto
 typing team sprint\, 3 days pre-show testing\, Southbank curatorial and pr
 oducing support\, and 20 hours of mentorship. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: R&D development through 2027\, presented at Creative Intelligence festi
 val\, Southbank Centre\, autumn 2027\n\nMore: https://www.costarnetwork.co
 .uk/calls/the-creative-intelligence-award?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.costarnetwork.co.uk/calls/the-creative-intelligence-award?u
 tm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:UK (lead applicant must be a UK-registered company\; project activ
 ity UK-based). Prototyping at Pinewood Studios\; showcase at Southbank Cen
 tre\, London.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cove-park-awarded-residencies-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261104T150945Z
DTSTART:20261211T163000Z
DTEND:20261211T170000Z
SUMMARY:Deadline 17:00 GMT: Cove Park Awarded Residencies 2027-2028 (Scotla
 nd\, UK\, Central and South America and diasporas)
DESCRIPTION:Closes 11 Dec 2026 at 17:00 GMT.\n\nCove Park's biennial call f
 or fully funded Awarded Residencies at its 50-acre rural residency site in
  Argyll\, on the west coast of Scotland\, programmed between May 2027 and 
 December 2028.\n\nOrganisation: Cove Park\nLocation: Cove Park\, Peaton Hi
 ll\, Argyll and Bute\, Scotland (in person\, private self-catering accommo
 dation and studio). Open to people based in the UK\, Central or South Amer
 ica\, Central and South American diaspora artists based in Europe or North
  America\, and Scotland-born artists living anywhere. Students in formal e
 ducation are not eligible.\nAward: Fully funded: GBP 775 per week fee (VAT
  inclusive\, freelance)\, travel allowance (Scotland GBP 65 to 200\, rest 
 of UK up to GBP 250\, Europe up to GBP 400\, other international up to GBP
  1\,250)\, materials up to GBP 150 for studio-based practitioners\, privat
 e accommodation and studio\, visa and access support. Application Access B
 ursary available (request by 30 November 2026). No application fee.\nDurat
 ion: 2 to 12 weeks (average 4)\, between May 2027 and December 2028\n\nMor
 e: https://covepark.org/awarded-residencies-2027-2028/?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://covepark.org/awarded-residencies-2027-2028/?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cove Park\, Peaton Hill\, Argyll and Bute\, Scotland (in person\, 
 private self-catering accommodation and studio). Open to people based in t
 he UK\, Central or South America\, Central and South American diaspora art
 ists based in Europe or North America\, and Scotland-born artists living a
 nywhere. Students in formal education are not eligible.
END:VEVENT
